course: dictionary
lesson: 3422
title: Extent: how big, how far
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The word extent is a noun. It tells you how big, long or wide something is, or how far something is true. «The Mole was staggered at the size, the extent, the ramifications of it all.» Staggered means amazed, and the clues are size and all of it, so extent must mean how big the whole thing is.
explain: The sentence goes on to list «passages, store-chambers, pillars, the arches, the pavements». Those are the parts that make up the whole, so the extent is the full area they cover. Ramifications means branches, more and more passages leading off from each other, so the extent is very large. Try your meaning in the sentence: «the size, the how big, the area» still makes sense.
explain: Extent also works for how far something is true. «To what extent do you agree?» asks how much you agree. The phrases «to some extent» means partly, and «to a large extent» means mostly. Swap in partly or mostly and you can check that you have understood it.
fact: Extent is a noun, and it is usually used in the singular.
fact: «The full extent of the forest» means the whole size or area of the forest.
fact: «To what extent» asks how far something is true or how much you agree.
fact: «To some extent» means partly, and «to a large extent» means mostly.
fact: «To such an extent that» means so much that: the damage was so big that something else happened.
exercise: «The full extent of the forest was hard to see from the hill.» What does extent mean here? | Its colour | * Its size or area | Its tallest tree | The name of the hill
exercise: Mum asks, «To what extent do you agree with the plan?» What is she asking? | Whether you have a plan | How loudly you agree | What time the plan starts | * How far you agree
exercise: «The rain damaged the roof to some extent.» What does «to some extent» mean? | Not at all | * Partly | Completely | Last week
test:
«The storm did damage to such an extent that the school had to close.» What does «to such an extent that» show? | Nothing was damaged at all | * The damage was so big that the school had to close | The school was painted | The storm was quiet
test:
In geography, the extent of a desert is its | * area, how much land it covers | temperature at noon | name of its tallest dune | number of people who visit it
exam:
Which phrase means mostly? | to some extent | not at all | * to a large extent | barely a whisper
